A brief overview of key terms:

`<package>`
    A package file or package name, depending on the command.

`<package@version>`
    The full package name consisting of the package name and version.

`--aarch64`
    Select the aarch64 processor architecture.

`--x86_64`
    Select the x86_64 processor architecture.


(1) INFO ABOUT UTILITY
=======================================================================================================

`catpkg` is the package manager for the CATPKG package system.

It is used to install, remove, verify, update, and inspect installed packages.
It also provides a command for building packages.

The package format used by catpkg is `.catpackage`.

Package metadata is stored according to the CATPKG metadata specification.


Permissions:

`install`
    Root privileges required.

`remove`
    Root privileges required.

`update`
    Root privileges required.

`verify`
    Default user permissions.

`database`
    Default user permissions.

`build`
    Default user permissions.

# (2) COMMANDS
=======================================================================================================

## (2.1) INSTALL
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`sudo catpkg install <path/to/package>`

Install or update the package located at the specified path.

Example:

`sudo catpkg install ./catpkg@2.0.0-beta.catpackage`

OR

Usage:

`sudo catpkg install <path/to/package_1> <path/to/package_2> <path/to/package_N>`

Install or update multiple packages located at the specified paths.

Example:

`sudo catpkg install ./filesystem@*.catpackage ./catstrap-bin@*.catpackage ./catstraplib@*.catpackage ./catpkg@*.catpackage`

OR

Usage:

`sudo catpkg install *.catpackage`

Install or update all `.catpackage` packages in the current directory.

Before installation, catpkg displays information about the packages
and asks for confirmation.

The command requires root privileges.


## (2.2) UPDATE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`sudo catpkg update <path/to/package>`

Update the package using the package file at the specified path.

Example:

`sudo catpkg update ./catpkg@2.0.0-beta.catpackage`

OR

Usage:

`sudo catpkg update <path/to/package_1> <path/to/package_2> <path/to/package_N>`

Update multiple packages using the package files at the specified paths.

Example:

`sudo catpkg update ./filesystem@*.catpackage ./catstrap-bin@*.catpackage ./catstraplib@*.catpackage ./catpkg@*.catpackage`

OR

Usage:

`sudo catpkg update *.catpackage`

Update all packages using the `.catpackage` files in the current directory.

Before updating, catpkg displays information about the packages
and asks for confirmation.

The command requires root privileges.


## (2.3) REMOVE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`sudo catpkg remove <path/to/package>`

Remove the package specified by the package file at the given path.

Example:

`sudo catpkg remove ./catpkg@2.0.0-beta.catpackage`

OR

Usage:

`sudo catpkg remove <path/to/package_1> <path/to/package_2> <path/to/package_N>`

Remove multiple packages specified by the package files at the given paths.

Example:

`sudo catpkg remove ./filesystem@*.catpackage ./catstrap-bin@*.catpackage ./catstraplib@*.catpackage ./catpkg@*.catpackage`

OR

Usage:

`sudo catpkg remove *.catpackage`

Remove all packages specified by the `.catpackage` files in the current directory.

Before removal, catpkg displays information about the packages
and asks for confirmation.

The command requires root privileges.


## (2.4) VERIFY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg verify <package>`

Verify the integrity of an installed package.

Example:

`catpkg verify catpkg`

If the package name matches multiple packages, catpkg displays
the available matches.

If multiple installed versions exist, catpkg asks for the full
package name:

`name@version`

The verification process uses package metadata and package integrity information.


## (2.5) DATABASE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg database list`

Display information about installed packages.

Example:

`catpkg database list`

The package list contains:

`Name`
    Package name.

`Version`
    Installed package version.

`Description`
    Package description from `PACKAGEINFO`.

`Dependencies`
    Package dependencies declared in `PACKAGEINFO`.


## (2.6) BUILD
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg build`

Build a package using the default architecture.

The target architecture can be selected explicitly:

`catpkg build --x86_64`

`catpkg build --aarch64`

The available architectures are:

`x86_64`
    64-bit x86 architecture.

`aarch64`
    64-bit ARM architecture.

When no architecture option is specified, the build process uses
the default architecture configuration.


## (2.7) HELP
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:

`catpkg help`

Display the catpkg command help.

Example:

`catpkg help`


# (3) PACKAGE NAME
=======================================================================================================

A full package name is written in the following form:

`name@version`

Example:

`catpkg@1.0.0-beta`

The package name and version are taken from the `PACKAGEINFO`
metadata stored inside the `.catpackage` file.


# (4) PACKAGE FILE
=======================================================================================================

A package installed by catpkg must use the `.catpackage` extension.

Example:

`catpkg-x86_64@1.0.0.catpackage`

Before installation or updating, catpkg checks the package format
and reads its `PACKAGEINFO`.

The metadata contains information such as:

* package name;
* package version;
* architecture;
* description;
* SHA;
* package size;
* dependencies;
* package operations.

The complete metadata format is documented separately in:

`CATPKG_METADATA_SPECIFICATION`


# (5) ARCHITECTURE OPTIONS
=======================================================================================================

Architecture options currently supported by the command-line interface:

`--x86_64`

```
Build for x86_64.
```

`--aarch64`

```
Build for aarch64.
```

These options are currently handled by:

`catpkg build`

Examples:

`catpkg build --x86_64`

`catpkg build --aarch64`

Architecture selection does not change the command itself;
it changes the target architecture passed to the build system.


# (6) ROOT PRIVILEGES
=======================================================================================================

The following commands require administrator privileges:

`catpkg install`

`catpkg update`

`catpkg remove`

When these commands are executed without root privileges,
catpkg prints an error and recommends running the command
with `sudo` or as root.

Example:

`sudo catpkg install ./package.catpackage`


